# zen-monitor-watcher.env — example config
#
# Copy to ~/.config/hypr/zen-monitor-watcher.env and uncomment the lines
# you want to override. Defaults are sane for ASUS ROG laptops; desktop
# users will typically only need to set ZEN_MONITOR_MAIN to their primary.
#
# After editing, restart the watcher:
#   systemctl --user restart zen-monitor-watcher.service

# ─── MAIN MONITOR (REQUIRED FOR DESKTOP USERS) ────────────────────────
# The "always-on" fallback. If a saved state would leave 0 monitors
# enabled, the watcher force-enables this one. Also re-enabled
# automatically when all externals disconnect (laptop scenario).
#
# Laptop default: eDP-1
# Desktop: pick your primary (DP-1, DP-2, HDMI-A-1 — check `hyprctl monitors`)

# ZEN_MONITOR_MAIN=eDP-1
# ZEN_MONITOR_MAIN=DP-1

# ─── FALLBACK MODE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Mode used when force-enabling MAIN. Format: "RESOLUTION,POSITION,SCALE"
# `preferred` lets the monitor pick its native resolution.
# Use specific values if your monitor doesn't pick the right native.

# ZEN_MONITOR_FALLBACK_MODE=preferred,auto,1
# ZEN_MONITOR_FALLBACK_MODE=2560x1600@165,auto,1.25
# ZEN_MONITOR_FALLBACK_MODE=3840x2160@60,auto,1.5

# ─── STATE STORAGE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where per-topology state files are saved. Default is fine for most.

# ZEN_MONITOR_STATE_DIR=$HOME/.config/hypr/zen-monitor-states

# ─── AUTO-SNAPSHOT DEBOUNCE ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Seconds to wait after the last monitor event before capturing the
# current state. Larger = more time to settle on a config; smaller =
# faster save. Default 10s is a good balance.

# ZEN_MONITOR_SNAPSHOT_DELAY=10

# ─── NOTIFICATIONS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Whether to fire notify-send on safety overrides + first capture.
# Set to 0 to silence (still logs to file).

# ZEN_MONITOR_NOTIFY=1

# ─── LOG FILE ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ZEN_MONITOR_LOG=/tmp/zen-monitor-watcher.log
